  <body>
    <title>
      <p>Marta L. Fixture</p>
      <p>The Lighthouse Keeper's Ledger</p>
    </title>
    <epigraph>
      <p>Every light is a promise made to someone you will never meet.</p>
      <text-author>Old harbour saying</text-author>
    </epigraph>
    <section id="part1">
      <title>
        <p>Part One</p>
        <p>The Island</p>
      </title>
      <section id="ch1">
        <title>
          <p>Chapter 1. Arrival</p>
        </title>
        <epigraph>
          <poem>
            <stanza>
              <v>The tide comes in, the tide goes out,</v>
              <v>and nobody asks it why.</v>
            </stanza>
          </poem>
          <text-author>From a sailor's notebook</text-author>
        </epigraph>
        <p>Anna stepped off the supply boat at noon, carrying a single suitcase and a letter of appointment that she had read <emphasis>eleven</emphasis> times on the crossing.</p>
        <p>The previous keeper, Mr. Hale, met her on the jetty. "You'll want to see the ledger first," he said, "before the lamp, before the stores, before anything."<a l:href="#n1" type="note">[1]</a></p>
        <p>She noticed that he said the word <strong>ledger</strong> the way other people say <strong><emphasis>church</emphasis></strong>.</p>
        <image l:href="#map.png"/>
        <p>The island was barely a mile across: a rock, a tower, a cottage, and a garden of stubborn cabbages.</p>
        <empty-line/>
        <p>That night the wind rose to force 8<a l:href="#n2" type="note">[2]</a>, and the lamp turned 2<sup>nd</sup> order lenses that had been polished, Hale said, every morning since 1871.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="ch2">
        <title>
          <p>Chapter 2. The Ledger</p>
        </title>
        <subtitle>* * *</subtitle>
        <p>The ledger was bound in green cloth. Each line held a date, a ship's name, a flag and a course, written in a neat hand that changed every few decades.</p>
        <cite>
          <p>14 March. <emphasis>Morning Star</emphasis>, Norwegian flag, bearing north-north-east. Passed safely.</p>
          <text-author>Ledger entry, page 212</text-author>
        </cite>
        <p>The date at the top of the page was <strikethrough>last year</strikethrough> next year.</p>
        <p>Hale tapped the margin, where someone had written the formula H<sub>2</sub>O and, beside it, the command <code>ring_bell(times=3)</code>.</p>
        <table>
          <tr>
            <th>Date</th>
            <th>Ship</th>
            <th>Status</th>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>14 March</td>
            <td>Morning Star</td>
            <td align="center">Passed safely</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>2 April</td>
            <td>Grey Heron</td>
            <td align="center">Not yet sailed</td>
          </tr>
        </table>
        <p>See also <a l:href="#ch3">Chapter 3</a> and the keeper's <a l:href="https://example.org/lighthouses">reference list</a>.</p>
      </section>
    </section>
    <section id="part2">
      <title>
        <p>Part Two</p>
      </title>
      <section id="ch3">
        <title>
          <p>Chapter 3. The Song of the Lamp</p>
        </title>
        <p>On her first solitary night, Anna found a poem pinned inside the lamp room door.</p>
        <poem>
          <title>
            <p>Lamp Room</p>
          </title>
          <epigraph>
            <p>For whoever comes after.</p>
          </epigraph>
          <stanza>
            <title>
              <p>I</p>
            </title>
            <v>I wind the clock, I trim the wick,</v>
            <v>I count the ships that pass;</v>
            <v>the sea writes faster than I can,</v>
            <v>its ink is wind and glass.</v>
          </stanza>
          <stanza>
            <title>
              <p>II</p>
            </title>
            <v>And if you read tomorrow's names</v>
            <v>before tomorrow came,</v>
            <v>do not be <emphasis>afraid</emphasis> of them:</v>
            <v>just keep the lamp the same.</v>
          </stanza>
          <text-author>E. Hale</text-author>
          <date>1952</date>
        </poem>
        <p>She read it twice, then opened the ledger and wrote the first line in her own hand.<a l:href="#n3" type="note">[3]</a></p>
      </section>
    </section>
  </body>
  <body name="notes">
    <title>
      <p>Notes</p>
    </title>
    <section id="n1">
      <title>
        <p>1</p>
      </title>
      <p>Keepers traditionally handed over the log book before the keys.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="n2">
      <title>
        <p>2</p>
      </title>
      <p>On the Beaufort scale, force 8 is a <emphasis>gale</emphasis>: 34 to 40 knots.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="n3">
      <title>
        <p>3</p>
      </title>
      <p>The entry reads: "25 November. All quiet. The light is kept."</p>
      <p>A second paragraph inside the same note, to test multi-paragraph notes.</p>
    </section>
